The problem asks us to determine the winner of a game played with an integer $n$.
Two players, Vanya and Vova, take turns. Vanya goes first.
In each turn, a player can add 1 or subtract 1 from the current integer.
Vanya wins if, after his move, the integer becomes divisible by 3.
If 10 moves have passed and Vanya has not won, Vova wins.
We need to determine the winner assuming optimal play.

Let's analyze the game.
The game state is defined by the current integer $x$.
Vanya moves first. He changes $x$ to $x+1$ or $x-1$.
If the new $x$ is divisible by 3, Vanya wins immediately.
If Vanya doesn't win, it becomes Vova's turn. Vova changes $x$ to $x+1$ or $x-1$.
The game ends after 10 moves total (5 moves by Vanya, 5 moves by Vova).
Wait, the problem says "If 10 moves have passed and Vanya has not won, then Vova wins."
