Vanya can choose $n+1$ or $n-1$.
If $n \equiv 0 \pmod 3$, then $n+1 \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ and $n-1 \equiv 2 \pmod 3$. Neither is divisible by 3.
So if $n \equiv 0 \pmod 3$, Vanya cannot win on his first move.
He must move to a number not divisible by 3.
Then Vova moves. Vova wants to prevent Vanya from winning later.
Vova can choose to move to a number such that Vanya cannot reach a multiple of 3 in the next turn.
Actually, let's look at the distance to the nearest multiple of 3.
Let $d = n \pmod 3$.
If $d=0$, distance is 0.
If $d=1$, distance is 1 (subtract 1).
If $d=2$, distance is 1 (add 1).
So, if $n \not\equiv 0 \pmod 3$, Vanya can win in 1 move.
Wait, if $n \equiv 1 \pmod 3$, Vanya subtracts 1 to get $n-1 \equiv 0 \pmod 3$. He wins.
If $n \equiv 2 \pmod 3$, Vanya adds 1 to get $n+1 \equiv 0 \pmod 3$. He wins.
So if $n \pmod 3 \neq 0$, Vanya wins immediately.

