The problem asks us to find the maximum possible bitwise OR of all elements in an array `nums` after applying an operation at most `k` times. The operation consists of choosing an element and multiplying it by 2.

Let $n$ be the length of `nums`.
Let the modified array be $nums'$.
We want to maximize $\bigvee_{i=0}^{n-1} nums'[i]$.
The operation allows us to multiply a specific element by 2. We can do this at most $k$ times in total.
Since multiplying by 2 is equivalent to a left shift by 1 ($x \times 2 = x \ll 1$), applying the operation $c$ times on an element $x$ results in $x \times 2^c$.
The total number of operations is the sum of operations applied to each element. Let $c_i$ be the number of times we apply the operation to $nums[i]$. Then $\sum c_i \le k$.
The value we want to maximize is $(nums[0] \times 2^{c_0}) \mid (nums[1] \times 2^{c_1}) \mid \dots \mid (nums[n-1] \times 2^{c_{n-1}})$.

Let's analyze the properties of bitwise OR.
The bitwise OR operation sets a bit to 1 if at least one of the operands has that bit set to 1.
To maximize the result, we generally want to set higher-order bits to 1.
Multiplying by 2 shifts bits to the left. This increases the value significantly.
Specifically, if we apply the operation $k$ times to a single element $nums[i]$, it becomes $nums[i] \times 2^k$.
If we distribute the $k$ operations among multiple elements, say $c_1$ times to $nums[i]$ and $c_2$ times to $nums[j]$, the contribution of $nums[i]$ becomes $nums[i] \times 2^{c_1}$ and $nums[j]$ becomes $nums[j] \times 2^{c_2}$.
Intuitively, shifting bits to the left is very powerful. $2^k$ is much larger than $2^{k-1} + 2^{k-2} + \dots$.
